1
00:00:01,760 --> 00:00:10,724
This is a simulation of Pong, the iconic 1970s arcade game, where you use this paddle to stop this ball from getting past you.

2
00:00:11,022 --> 00:00:19,040
It's a pretty simple game for us humans to learn, and as of quite recently, with advancements in AI, computers can learn it too.

3
00:00:19,280 --> 00:00:25,280
But today, Pong has a new kind of player, not a computer, nor a human.

4
00:00:25,520 --> 00:00:30,720
A cluster of living human brain cells on top of a silicon chip can play Pong.

5
00:00:30,990 --> 00:00:31,440
Yep.

6
00:00:31,680 --> 00:00:38,640
Researchers in a lab taught a tiny clump of neurons to, in a sense, play this video game.

7
00:00:39,840 --> 00:00:41,920
But it's more than just a game.

8
00:00:42,160 --> 00:00:49,360
Some believe merging biology with computing could up-end our approach to artificial intelligence.

9
00:00:49,680 --> 00:00:57,440
AI is a massive, often volatile global market with people looking all over for a competitive edge.

10
00:00:57,680 --> 00:01:05,200
In 2025, private sector companies teamed up to invest more than $500 billion in AI infrastructure alone.

11
00:01:05,760 --> 00:01:15,120
But what if the building blocks of that infrastructure include living human brain cells with the promises and challenges of our own biology?

12
00:01:15,360 --> 00:01:16,960
Sounds like sci-fi, right?

13
00:01:17,120 --> 00:01:18,960
Well, we're kind of on our way.

14
00:01:19,278 --> 00:01:21,120
Science fiction talked about this.

15
00:01:21,280 --> 00:01:23,520
I love thinking about the Hollywood stuff.

16
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:25,840
Like, are we really computers?

17
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:27,920
Are computers really human?

18
00:01:28,240 --> 00:01:34,000
My life has been focused on how can you elicit intelligence from brain cells in a dish.

19
00:01:56,800 --> 00:02:01,840
Brett Kagan is the chief scientific officer at Cortical Labs in Melbourne, Australia.

20
00:02:02,094 --> 00:02:07,012
They made a name for themselves when they got their neurons to play Pong in 2022.

21
00:02:07,342 --> 00:02:09,120
They called it Dishbrain.

22
00:02:10,094 --> 00:02:12,320
Yeah, let's just set up and see how it goes.

23
00:02:12,480 --> 00:02:14,480
Maybe we'll all be in for a surprise.

24
00:02:16,240 --> 00:02:17,360
Quick timeout.

25
00:02:18,350 --> 00:02:26,660
Before we go any further, it might be worth understanding why companies like Cortical Labs are blending living neurons with synthetic systems.

26
00:02:29,550 --> 00:02:36,836
Compared to traditional machine learning, brains need far less training data to understand real-life environments and new situations.

27
00:02:37,454 --> 00:02:43,908
So what you're seeing here is we're investigating can neurons actually distinguish between different handwritten digits?

28
00:02:44,142 --> 00:02:52,516
And we want to see can the neurons behave in a way that says they recognize that all of the sixes look like sixes, all of the fours look like fours, and so on.

29
00:02:55,630 --> 00:03:02,820
Anything with biology can learn to navigate its environment incredibly quickly, incredibly efficiently.

30
00:03:03,150 --> 00:03:07,280
And we've had to learn to do that through evolution, otherwise we died.

31
00:03:11,420 --> 00:03:17,420
The promise of far less data and energy consumption is the holy grail for AI advancement.

32
00:03:18,200 --> 00:03:18,840
Here's why.

33
00:03:19,440 --> 00:03:25,360
Supercomputers like this one can be a million times more powerful than the fastest laptop.

34
00:03:25,600 --> 00:03:31,760
But both supercomputers and AI run on the same basic technology, silicon chips.

35
00:03:32,080 --> 00:03:36,000
There's never been a focus like there is currently on the chip industry.

36
00:03:36,160 --> 00:03:39,360
People have realized that chips are the heart of everything.

37
00:03:39,600 --> 00:03:51,840
That this fundamental change to the economy that's being promised in the form of AI depends upon semiconductors, depends upon their rapid development, and depends upon an ever-increasing supply of them.

38
00:03:52,590 --> 00:04:00,320
For over 50 years, we have been creating better chips by backing more and more transistors inside one integrated circuit.

39
00:04:00,560 --> 00:04:05,840
The transistors are on-off switches, the physical manifestation of ones and zeros.

40
00:04:06,160 --> 00:04:10,480
And we've gotten faster and faster by going smaller and smaller.

41
00:04:10,880 --> 00:04:17,760
Over the last six decades, we've been trying to double the amount of transistors on an integrated circuit every two years.

42
00:04:18,240 --> 00:04:23,680
This is the heralded Moore's law, observed by this guy back in 1975.

43
00:04:24,400 --> 00:04:29,360
Today, a phone's processing chip contains billions of transistors.

44
00:04:29,520 --> 00:04:35,236
And those transistors are so small, they're just a few nanometers apart in places.

45
00:04:35,680 --> 00:04:44,676
But, as our good old Intel co-founder, Gordon Moore, observed, this steady march of progress also has an endpoint.

46
00:04:45,358 --> 00:04:52,868
You're creating a chip, a three dimensional circuit by depositing layers of material on a disk of silicon.

47
00:04:53,454 --> 00:04:56,000
Some of those layers now are one atom thick.

48
00:04:56,270 --> 00:04:56,720
Guess what?

49
00:04:56,942 --> 00:04:58,948
When you get to one atom, you can't go any thinner.

50
00:04:59,776 --> 00:05:06,016
So, we have a problem, and folks all over the world are looking for solutions to our processing limits.

51
00:05:06,176 --> 00:05:10,096
After all, our demand for more computing likely isn't slowing down.

52
00:05:10,416 --> 00:05:16,196
It's not like suddenly companies and governments and organizations are going to say, you know what, we don't need more data.

53
00:05:16,496 --> 00:05:18,816
Everybody wants to collect more and more data, right?

54
00:05:18,976 --> 00:05:21,376
People liken it to the, you know, modern gold rush.

55
00:05:21,776 --> 00:05:23,456
I wonder whether DeepSeek has kind of changed.

56
00:05:23,536 --> 00:05:26,176
I mean, it feels like it's up-ended the race this week.

57
00:05:26,416 --> 00:05:29,776
DeepSeak is such a fundamental change to the economics of what's going on.

58
00:05:30,016 --> 00:05:36,004
DeepSeek's January 2025 effect on markets was a strong reminder of these economic incentives.

59
00:05:36,494 --> 00:05:43,396
Companies are racing to build more efficient AI models all around the world, at times also fueling existing political tensions.

60
00:05:43,790 --> 00:05:49,856
The president said that he believes that this is a wake-up call to the American AI industry.

61
00:05:50,190 --> 00:05:55,844
One long-standing breakthrough in AI comes from biology and our ability to replicate it.

62
00:05:56,206 --> 00:05:57,412
Back to neurons.

63
00:06:22,958 --> 00:06:26,692
And that's where sort of these ideas of like artificial neural networks and deep learning came from.

64
00:06:26,976 --> 00:06:33,252
And the current status of these artificial neural networks and deep learning is pretty fantastic.

65
00:06:35,216 --> 00:06:42,724
But building these AI models and allowing masses of people to use them is costing a lot of energy.

66
00:06:43,536 --> 00:06:51,456
Already there are parts of the world where there literally isn't enough power available via the grid to support these giant data centers.

67
00:06:52,366 --> 00:07:00,708
By 2034, data centers around the world are expected to consume roughly 1,580 terawatt hours yearly.

68
00:07:01,136 --> 00:07:04,836
That is about as much as is used by all of India.

69
00:07:22,126 --> 00:07:26,416
So neurons might help solve some problems, especially around energy.

70
00:07:26,862 --> 00:07:31,684
Our brains are much better and more efficient in similar tasks to AI.

71
00:07:32,976 --> 00:07:38,336
For example, a supercomputer must consume up to 40 megawatts of power to function.

72
00:07:38,496 --> 00:07:41,092
Our brains run on just 20 watts.

73
00:07:41,486 --> 00:07:45,856
That's the difference between a couple of LED light bulbs and thousands of homes.

74
00:07:48,560 --> 00:07:55,940
Our brains are also powerful, with about 86 billion neurons that form over 100 trillion connections to each other.

75
00:07:56,976 --> 00:08:02,180
And with cells, the distinction between hardware and software isn't quite as well distinct.

76
00:08:02,416 --> 00:08:04,516
They're dynamic and intertwined.

77
00:08:04,878 --> 00:08:08,580
The cell's actual structure can change and mutate too.

78
00:08:09,646 --> 00:08:15,616
Because of the way that computer chips are organized, they have limited logic functions.

79
00:08:15,696 --> 00:08:18,096
They tend to want to do things one thing after another.

80
00:08:18,176 --> 00:08:20,496
That's what they're good at, that's what they were designed to do.

81
00:08:20,814 --> 00:08:22,096
Human brain doesn't work that way.

82
00:08:22,176 --> 00:08:29,216
The human brain is kind of a lattice structure of interconnections where information flows in sort of different ways and different points.

83
00:08:29,376 --> 00:08:33,412
So the limitations of bio is that we're just at the very beginning of it.

84
00:08:40,174 --> 00:08:42,212
Dishbrain was our first prototype.

85
00:08:42,958 --> 00:08:51,588
We were essentially trying to ask the question: can you interact with a bunch of brain cells in a dish and get them to do anything useful at all?

86
00:08:54,638 --> 00:09:00,580
Brett and his team started by growing a layer of 800,000 neurons on top of a silicon chip.

87
00:09:01,070 --> 00:09:04,036
That's about the size of a small bumblebee's brain.

88
00:09:04,654 --> 00:09:16,644
And since neurons in our brain naturally communicate using electrical impulses, the researchers could both send and receive impulses between the neurons and a computer hooked up to a silicon chip.

89
00:09:17,390 --> 00:09:25,616
To translate the Pong game into something this clump of neurons could sense and control, the chip is divided into three sections.

90
00:09:25,966 --> 00:09:34,496
There's one larger sensory section, which sends electrical impulses to the neurons to represent aspects of the game, like where the ball is.

91
00:09:34,766 --> 00:09:36,576
There's also two motor sections.

92
00:09:36,878 --> 00:09:39,684
If neurons send impulses in this one, the paddle moves up.

93
00:09:40,096 --> 00:09:43,616
If neurons send impulses in the other, the paddle moves down.

94
00:09:44,238 --> 00:09:48,100
The chip acts as a communicator between the neurons and the game.

95
00:09:48,654 --> 00:09:52,516
But it still doesn't mean that these neurons would want to play the game.

96
00:09:53,102 --> 00:09:58,180
Why would a bunch of cells in a dish actually change their activity in any particular way?

97
00:09:58,638 --> 00:10:04,612
There's no pong-playing gene that exists in brain cells to make them want to play the game pong.

98
00:10:06,062 --> 00:10:09,376
So the researchers tried to give the neurons incentive.

99
00:10:09,646 --> 00:10:16,932
Every time the brain cells missed the ball with the paddle, the researchers stimulated them with chaotic and unpredictable bouts of electricity.

100
00:10:17,422 --> 00:10:23,556
On the other hand, when the neurons successfully hit the ball, they received predictable stimuli from the researchers.

101
00:10:23,886 --> 00:10:27,684
Within minutes, the neurons started hitting the pong ball more consistently.

102
00:10:28,206 --> 00:10:29,696
Why did it work?

103
00:10:30,062 --> 00:10:34,404
One possible explanation is a theory called the free energy principle.

104
00:10:35,022 --> 00:10:44,612
It suggests that almost all things in nature, even as small as the dishbrain, are constantly trying to perceive and understand their world and minimize surprise.

105
00:10:45,210 --> 00:10:54,370
If you can't predict what's going on in the world, if you can't say if I reach for a glass of water I'll successfully pick the water up, it's very hard to survive.

106
00:10:55,536 --> 00:11:06,852
Dishbrain was a proof of concept for this larger idea of biocomputing, also called organic computing, biological hardware, and perhaps most vividly, wetware.

107
00:11:07,438 --> 00:11:17,216
Dishbrain made a splash, so big in fact, that another team of scientists tried the same experiment using hydrogel, a non-living, soft, flexible substance similar to jello.

108
00:11:17,710 --> 00:11:20,068
The hydrogel also learned to play pong.

109
00:11:20,496 --> 00:11:25,536
So the question became: what can neurons do that other substrates can't?

110
00:11:25,774 --> 00:11:28,176
How much computing abilities can they provide?

111
00:11:28,256 --> 00:11:31,296
And frankly, can they make any money now?

112
00:11:38,062 --> 00:11:38,416
Vervet?

113
00:11:39,022 --> 00:11:40,644
It's a small city, it's peaceful.

114
00:11:49,936 --> 00:11:51,268
Meet Fred Jordan.

115
00:11:51,822 --> 00:11:58,496
Fred's the CEO and founder of Final Spark, a biotech company nestled in the quaint Swiss town of Vervey.

116
00:11:58,990 --> 00:12:02,916
Final Spark is a deep tech startup with a team of just under 10 people.

117
00:12:03,374 --> 00:12:06,736
Deep tech startups don't focus on end user services.

118
00:12:06,896 --> 00:12:11,460
Instead, they aim to solve complex problems by harnessing scientific discoveries.

119
00:12:11,886 --> 00:12:16,548
Like Cortical Labs, Final Spark is also a biocomputing company.

120
00:12:17,262 --> 00:12:25,412
We have a way to have neurons connect together to create a small bowl that is called a brain organoid.

121
00:12:27,342 --> 00:12:31,600
Final Spark relies on their own funds of just over $1.5 million.

122
00:12:32,160 --> 00:12:40,160
Cortical Labs, in comparison, raised around $10 million in 2023 from folks like Incutel and Horizon Ventures.

123
00:12:40,480 --> 00:12:43,844
Final Spark says they have not accepted outside investment.

124
00:12:44,238 --> 00:12:53,636
They've used their own money towards research and development and made 16 brain organoids available for a subscription fee on a cloud computing network called the NeuroPlatform.

125
00:12:54,000 --> 00:13:01,828
You can come and you will look at real-time information streamed 24-7 from our neurons here in Switzerland.

126
00:13:02,320 --> 00:13:07,460
This picture here shows a close-up version of what's inside the incubator.

127
00:13:07,950 --> 00:13:12,356
The important part for us is this activity.

128
00:13:12,718 --> 00:13:18,756
Each of this small graph here represents the voltage for one second of one electrode.

129
00:13:19,310 --> 00:13:30,720
Now people who are using the neuroplatform are able to also send some stimulation to some of these electrodes, to which sometimes they will answer.

130
00:13:50,480 --> 00:13:52,644
Initially, we built this only for us.

131
00:13:52,880 --> 00:14:01,796
And then one university came and then nine and then 34, and now we have 200 people who want to access remotely to our brain organoids.

132
00:14:05,614 --> 00:14:06,692
This is totally new.

133
00:14:06,800 --> 00:14:08,100
There is no background at all.

134
00:14:08,622 --> 00:14:12,720
So there are more questions than uh than facts about this way of investing.

135
00:14:12,800 --> 00:14:15,840
So I think this is the biggest challenge.

136
00:14:16,080 --> 00:14:29,600
Investors are always cautious, fundamentally about deep tech. And it's even not only deep tech. it's a new deep tech. If you talk about quantum computing, for instance, it's deep tech, but it's an old, deep tech. In the future.

137
00:14:29,760 --> 00:14:37,920
we'll have to live in a world where there will be some parts in computing, which are living and other, which are just artificial.

138
00:14:48,270 --> 00:14:54,640
It's all well and good for research purposes, but a future where biology and hardware become one isn't easy.

139
00:14:54,958 --> 00:14:59,972
Cells need to be fed, kept a comfortable 37 degrees Celsius, their waste cleaned.

140
00:15:00,206 --> 00:15:01,892
After a while, they die.

141
00:15:03,040 --> 00:15:05,892
You have to look at the environmental physics.

142
00:15:06,734 --> 00:15:10,244
A computer chip now is drawing multiple hundreds of watts.

143
00:15:10,574 --> 00:15:19,120
That throws off so much heat that you have to have these cooling systems in place, and that's that's a tremendous problem for the computer industry.

144
00:15:19,374 --> 00:15:26,160
But imagine trying to bring in biological materials into that environment if you're going to make a hybrid type technology.

145
00:15:26,240 --> 00:15:27,268
It's just not possible.

146
00:15:27,534 --> 00:15:30,788
You literally are going to start cooking the biological material.

147
00:15:30,960 --> 00:15:37,280
Any number of engineering problems exist that will need to be solved if we are to bring in this medium.

148
00:15:38,880 --> 00:15:41,360
All this is hard to scale.

149
00:15:42,126 --> 00:15:45,920
Investors who take the sleep now aren't looking to turn a quick profit.

150
00:15:47,120 --> 00:15:52,480
This is Cortical Labs' first commercial product, the CL1 unit.

151
00:15:52,640 --> 00:15:55,520
It's a processor with human brain cells in it.

152
00:15:55,760 --> 00:16:06,240
CL1s come with either neurons on a chip, organoids, or a type of neural material Cortical Labs calls bioengineered intelligence.

153
00:16:07,182 --> 00:16:14,148
This box acts like a body for the cells, feeding them, disposing of their waste, filtrating and pumping fluid around to keep them alive.

154
00:16:14,990 --> 00:16:20,000
The company's success hinges on whether or not they sell their CL1 units.

155
00:16:20,240 --> 00:16:25,840
Each one goes for around $35,000, and they're expected to launch in March 2025.

156
00:16:26,670 --> 00:16:29,924
Scaling is always a challenge, just logistically.

157
00:16:30,254 --> 00:16:38,020
It's one thing to build a prototype in the lab, it's another to have hundreds of them rolling off a production line and have them be reliable and stable.

158
00:16:45,806 --> 00:16:47,136
I'm not the Pope.

159
00:16:47,296 --> 00:16:48,176
I can fail.

160
00:16:48,398 --> 00:16:56,496
Perhaps my imagination, my fantasy is not big enough, but I would not invest into these companies from my side.

161
00:16:56,846 --> 00:16:58,468
This is Thomas Harting.

162
00:16:58,656 --> 00:17:03,684
He focuses on toxicology, think poisons, in an effort to improve public health.

163
00:17:04,096 --> 00:17:06,052
Thomas, how much time do you spend in here?

164
00:17:06,336 --> 00:17:11,908
Honestly, I come here when I look for my wife or some of the other people in our team.

165
00:17:12,526 --> 00:17:18,976
He spends a large amount of his time thinking about how we can use biocomputing and these brain organoids now.

166
00:17:20,398 --> 00:17:23,140
Our group was not the first to produce brain organoids.

167
00:17:23,456 --> 00:17:27,812
We used to call them mini-brains at the time, but we were the first to standardize this.

168
00:17:28,526 --> 00:17:39,136
And we were very proud that when we showed this at one of these big science science journalist events, the AAAS, we got on the front page of the Financial Times.

169
00:17:42,030 --> 00:17:43,056
I'm a crazy professor.

170
00:17:43,136 --> 00:17:44,176
I can't do what I want.

171
00:17:44,302 --> 00:17:46,148
I'm trying to replace animal testing.

172
00:17:47,150 --> 00:17:52,484
Animal studies aren't always great at predicting how toxins or poisons affect humans.

173
00:17:52,782 --> 00:18:01,284
And although human brain organoids today deliver pretty modest computing tricks, their promise in the field of biomedicine is already remarkable.

174
00:18:02,030 --> 00:18:04,644
The potential for just basic neurobiology is enormous.

175
00:18:05,056 --> 00:18:08,516
It could be a very good strategy to accelerate drug development.

176
00:18:08,814 --> 00:18:12,676
It is a lengthy process to do an animal experiment, not only a costly one.

177
00:18:13,262 --> 00:18:19,456
The value of being one day earlier to the market for a pharmaceutical company is about $1 million.

178
00:18:34,062 --> 00:18:42,816
The aim of the lab is to understand Parkinson's disease and we were pretty unsatisfied with available animal models as well as more simple cell culture models.

179
00:18:43,246 --> 00:18:52,868
The connection between the biocomputing and the disease modeling is actually quite interesting because one of the features or characteristics that we see in neurodegenerative diseases is dementia.

180
00:18:53,006 --> 00:18:55,296
So people start to forget things.

181
00:18:56,142 --> 00:19:05,252
The concept that we would like to explore is if I can train a brain organoid to learn certain tasks, can it also forget these tasks?

182
00:19:05,710 --> 00:19:10,948
And would it forget these tasks quicker or in a different way if it's in a disease context?

183
00:19:11,216 --> 00:19:16,736
Like, would an Alzheimer brain organoid forget things quicker than a healthy control organoid?

184
00:19:19,216 --> 00:19:24,656
There are no cures for neurodegenerative diseases, only symptom management.

185
00:19:25,070 --> 00:19:28,932
What if we could understand and get rid of the disease itself?

186
00:19:29,216 --> 00:19:48,676
If we can really understand the human brain, then we can potentially treat Alzheimer's, Lou Gehrig's disease, epilepsy, all kinds of brain-based disease that we really don't have good treatments for now.

187
00:19:50,576 --> 00:19:57,508
Many pharma companies have started actually to move away from these kinds of diseases because it was just not commercially viable.

188
00:19:58,062 --> 00:20:10,016
Now, with these new technologies coming up, I think there's kind of new hopes that we would really develop or be able to develop therapies for these diseases that burden our societies quite a lot.

189
00:20:11,982 --> 00:20:22,052
Studies suggest that at some point in their lives, more than one third of the entire human population will suffer from neurological conditions like Parkinson's, Alzheimer's, or dementia.

190
00:20:22,656 --> 00:20:31,364
By 2050, some project there will be an overall 22% increase in brain disorders, in part fuelled by population growth and aging.

191
00:20:36,814 --> 00:20:41,188
This is direct and indirect costs, so really like the healthcare, but also what it does to the economy.

192
00:20:41,456 --> 00:20:44,996
At the moment, there is about 10 million Parkinson's patients in the world.

193
00:20:45,422 --> 00:20:49,572
Yearly in the US, there is about 90,000 people being diagnosed with PD.

194
00:20:50,016 --> 00:20:55,556
And since worldwide populations are aging, the likelihood to suffer from PD is increasing.

195
00:20:58,030 --> 00:21:09,316
The idea of using biological medium to either process or store information makes sense in a lot of ways, but there are those who's saying actually this is living tissue.

196
00:21:09,710 --> 00:21:13,124
Not only how can we actually do this, but should we be doing this?

197
00:21:13,632 --> 00:21:18,592
I think the biggest elephant in the room is would a brain organoid become self-aware.

198
00:21:21,422 --> 00:21:30,992
I'm not worried at all at the moment with these tiny, tiny little balls of cells that they would suddenly develop something which I could consider consciousness.

199
00:21:31,152 --> 00:21:37,380
There's a lot of ethical issues in all biomedical research, and this starts if you're using stem cells, as we do.

200
00:21:37,742 --> 00:21:42,192
You have to imagine the donors of these cells are often still alive.

201
00:21:43,632 --> 00:21:46,672
Is your signature still valid?

202
00:21:46,832 --> 00:21:51,300
Because you couldn't imagine that somebody would produce a brain which is thinking.

203
00:21:51,694 --> 00:21:53,632
These are all interesting questions, yeah?

204
00:21:53,966 --> 00:22:00,772
And they really get hot if we assume at some point in the future these brain organisms could suffer.

205
00:22:01,166 --> 00:22:05,072
They could experience pain, they could suffer from what they learn.

206
00:22:05,550 --> 00:22:06,752
So imagine this, yeah.

